/* LLMOps and Production AI — 44 questions */
window.QA_SECTION = {
  id: '08',
  slug: 'llmops',
  title: 'LLMOps & Production AI',
  blurb: 'Serving, quantization, observability, cost control, and keeping LLM systems alive under load.',
  questions: [
{ q: 'How does prompt caching work?', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `Prompt caching stores the computed KV cache for a prompt <em>prefix</em> so subsequent requests sharing that prefix skip prefill for those tokens entirely.<br><br><strong>The mechanism:</strong> prefill computes key and value vectors for every prompt token. If the next request starts with the identical token sequence, those K/V tensors are still valid — causal attention means earlier tokens never depend on later ones. So the server keeps them (in GPU memory or offloaded) keyed by a hash of the prefix, and a matching request resumes from the divergence point.<br><br><strong>The impact is large:</strong> up to ~90% input cost reduction on the cached portion and a substantial TTFT drop, since prefill is the dominant cost for long prompts.<br><br><strong>The critical implication is prompt ordering.</strong> Caching is <em>prefix</em>-based, so it only works if the static content comes first: system prompt → tool definitions → few-shot examples → then variable content (retrieved chunks, user query). Putting the user query first invalidates the cache on every request. Reordering a prompt for cache-friendliness is often the cheapest large cost win available, and it requires no quality tradeoff.<br><br><strong>Practical constraints:</strong> caches have a TTL (typically minutes), a minimum prefix length, and exact-match semantics — one changed token invalidates everything after it. Some providers charge a small premium for cache writes, so it pays off only with reuse.<br><br><strong>Second-order:</strong> context compaction invalidates the prefix, so compact in batches rather than every step.` },

{ q: 'Explain prefill vs decode.', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `Generation has two phases with <strong>opposite bottlenecks</strong>, and nearly every serving optimization follows from this split.<br><br><strong>Prefill</strong> processes the entire prompt in one forward pass, computing the KV cache for all prompt tokens in parallel. It is <em>compute-bound</em> — large matrix multiplies saturating the GPU's tensor cores — and its cost scales with prompt length (quadratically for attention). It determines <strong>time-to-first-token</strong>.<br><br><strong>Decode</strong> generates one token at a time. Each step does very little arithmetic (a single token's worth) but must stream the entire model weights plus the accumulated KV cache from HBM. It is <em>memory-bandwidth-bound</em> and determines <strong>tokens per second</strong>.<br><br><strong>Why this matters operationally:</strong> the two phases need different optimizations. TTFT improves with prompt caching, chunked prefill, and shorter prompts. Throughput improves with continuous batching, GQA, quantized KV cache, and speculative decoding — none of which help TTFT much.<br><br><strong>The scheduling conflict:</strong> naively interleaving them means a long prefill stalls every in-flight decode, spiking inter-token latency for other users. Fixes are <em>chunked prefill</em> (split long prompts into pieces interleaved with decode steps) or <em>disaggregated serving</em> (separate GPU pools per phase, transferring KV cache between them), which lets you size and scale each independently.<br><br><strong>The arithmetic-intensity framing:</strong> decode at batch size 1 has terrible arithmetic intensity — you read gigabytes of weights to do one token's math. Batching is what fixes it, which is why throughput and latency trade off.` },

{ q: 'Explain the AI product lifecycle from ideation to production.', tags: [], a: `<strong>1. Problem framing.</strong> Decide whether an LLM is the right tool at all, and define what success means in measurable terms before building. The most common failure is skipping this and discovering months later that nobody agreed what "good" meant.<br><br><strong>2. Eval set first.</strong> Build a labeled evaluation set from realistic examples <em>before</em> writing prompts. Without it, every subsequent decision is guesswork and every improvement is anecdotal.<br><br><strong>3. Prototype.</strong> Prompt engineering against the eval set. Establish a baseline with the best model, then find the cheapest configuration that meets the bar.<br><br><strong>4. Ground and augment.</strong> Add RAG or tools where the gap is knowledge or capability, not phrasing.<br><br><strong>5. Harden.</strong> Guardrails, structured output validation, error handling, fallbacks, rate limits, and cost controls. This is where most of the engineering actually lives.<br><br><strong>6. Deploy incrementally.</strong> Shadow mode (run in parallel, log, do not serve), then canary, then A/B against the current experience. Never a full cutover on a system whose behavior is statistical.<br><br><strong>7. Observe and iterate.</strong> Trace everything, mine production failures back into the eval set, and treat that loop as the actual product development process.<br><br><strong>What differs from traditional software:</strong> behavior is probabilistic, so testing is statistical rather than deterministic; quality degrades silently rather than throwing errors; and the model can change underneath you. That last point is why version pinning and continuous evaluation are not optional.` },

{ q: 'What is LLMOps, and how does it differ from MLOps?', tags: ['FREQUENTLY ASKED'], a: `LLMOps is the operational practice around LLM-based applications. It shares MLOps' concerns — versioning, deployment, monitoring, evaluation — but several assumptions break.<br><br><strong>What changes:</strong><br>• <em>You usually do not train the model.</em> The artifact you version and iterate on is the <em>prompt</em>, the retrieval corpus, and the tool definitions — not model weights. Prompts need the same rigor as code: version control, review, regression tests.<br>• <em>Evaluation is largely subjective.</em> Traditional ML has ground-truth labels and accuracy. LLM output quality often requires LLM judges or human review, which are themselves noisy and need calibration.<br>• <em>Cost is per-request and variable.</em> A traditional model has fixed inference cost; an LLM's cost depends on prompt and output length, so cost monitoring and token budgets are first-class operational concerns.<br>• <em>Non-determinism.</em> The same input can produce different output, so tests must be statistical — pass rates over multiple runs, not exact assertions.<br>• <em>The model is a third-party dependency</em> that can change or be deprecated. Version pinning and provider redundancy become reliability requirements.<br>• <em>New failure modes:</em> hallucination, prompt injection, and jailbreaks have no MLOps analogue.<br><br><strong>What stays the same:</strong> data quality determines outcomes, monitoring beats hoping, incremental rollout, and reproducibility.<br><br><strong>The honest framing:</strong> LLMOps is less "a new discipline" than MLOps with the training loop removed and the evaluation problem made much harder.` },

{ q: 'How do you serve LLMs in production?', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `<strong>The first decision is API versus self-hosted.</strong> API for speed to market and frontier capability; self-hosted for data control, version pinning, cost at volume, and latency guarantees. Most teams should start on an API — self-hosting before you know your workload is premature.<br><br><strong>For self-hosting, use a purpose-built inference server</strong> — vLLM, SGLang, TensorRT-LLM — not a naive <code>model.generate()</code> loop. The gap is roughly an order of magnitude in throughput, and it comes from three things:<br>• <em>Continuous batching</em> — admit and evict sequences at each decode step rather than waiting for the whole batch to finish, keeping the GPU saturated under variable-length generation.<br>• <em>PagedAttention</em> — KV cache in fixed-size blocks like OS virtual memory, eliminating fragmentation that otherwise wastes most of the cache, and enabling prefix sharing across requests.<br>• <em>Optimized kernels</em> — Flash Attention, fused operations, quantized inference.<br><br><strong>Sizing:</strong> KV cache memory usually determines max concurrency, not compute. Available VRAM after weights ÷ per-sequence cache size = concurrent sequences.<br><br><strong>Around the server:</strong> a gateway for auth, rate limiting, and routing; streaming responses; and admission control rather than unbounded queues.<br><br><strong>The autoscaling problem to name:</strong> GPU cold starts are minutes because you load tens of GB of weights. Reactive scaling does not work — keep warm capacity, scale on queue depth with lead time, and shed load gracefully at the ceiling.` },

{ q: 'What is model quantization?', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `Quantization stores and computes model weights (and sometimes activations) at lower numerical precision — fp16/bf16 → int8 → int4 — reducing memory and increasing throughput.<br><br><strong>Why it works so well for LLMs:</strong> decode is memory-bandwidth-bound, not compute-bound. Halving the bytes read per token nearly doubles decode speed, independent of any arithmetic savings. Quantization is therefore a latency win, not just a memory win.<br><br><strong>The main methods:</strong><br>• <em>GPTQ</em> — post-training, layer-by-layer weight quantization minimizing output error using second-order information. Fast to apply, needs a small calibration set.<br>• <em>AWQ</em> — protects the small fraction of "salient" weight channels identified from activation magnitudes, quantizing the rest aggressively. Often better quality than GPTQ at 4-bit.<br>• <em>GGUF/llama.cpp</em> — the CPU and consumer-hardware ecosystem, with a range of k-quant variants.<br>• <em>fp8</em> — hardware-native on H100+, near-lossless, and increasingly the production default.<br>• <em>QAT</em> — quantization-aware training, best quality, requires a training run.<br><br><strong>The quality curve:</strong> 8-bit is essentially lossless. 4-bit is usually acceptable with a modern method. Below 4-bit degrades sharply. Larger models tolerate quantization better than small ones.<br><br><strong>The caveat that matters:</strong> perplexity barely moves while task performance can drop noticeably, particularly on reasoning and long-context tasks. Always evaluate on your actual task, not on a perplexity number.` },

{ q: 'How do you monitor LLM applications in production?', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `<strong>Four layers, and skipping the quality layer is the classic mistake</strong> — LLM systems degrade silently rather than throwing errors.<br><br><strong>1. System metrics:</strong> latency (TTFT and total, p50/p95/p99), throughput, error rate by type, and availability. Distinguish provider errors, timeouts, rate limits, and content-filter blocks — they need different responses.<br><br><strong>2. Cost metrics:</strong> tokens in/out per request, cost per request, and cost attributed by feature, user, and tenant. Watch the <em>distribution</em>, not the mean — cost is heavily long-tailed and a few runaway requests usually drive the bill.<br><br><strong>3. Quality metrics — the hard and essential part:</strong> sampled LLM-judge scores on live traffic, groundedness/citation-validity for RAG, schema-validation failure rate for structured output, guardrail trigger rates, and refusal rate. Parse-failure rate is an excellent early warning: it spikes immediately on a model change or prompt regression.<br><br><strong>4. User signals:</strong> thumbs up/down, regeneration rate, conversation abandonment, escalation to human. Regeneration rate is underrated — it is a strong implicit quality signal that costs nothing to collect.<br><br><strong>Alert on the right things:</strong> quality drift is gradual, so alert on <em>trend</em> against a baseline rather than absolute thresholds. Cost and error spikes warrant immediate alerts.<br><br><strong>The operational requirement underneath all of it:</strong> log full traces — prompt, retrieved context, model version, output — so any alert is investigable. Metrics tell you something broke; traces tell you what.` },

{ q: 'What is LLM observability?', tags: [], a: `LLM observability is the ability to understand <em>why</em> a system produced a given output — not just that it was slow or errored. It extends conventional tracing to capture the semantic content of each step.<br><br><strong>The core unit is the trace:</strong> a full request captured as a tree of spans — query rewriting, retrieval (with chunk ids and scores), reranking, prompt assembly, model call (with the fully rendered prompt, model version, parameters, tokens, latency), tool calls, and the final output.<br><br><strong>Why the rendered prompt matters more than anything else:</strong> most "the model ignored my instruction" bugs are actually rendering bugs — an empty variable, a truncated context, a malformed chat template. Logging the template instead of the rendered string makes these undebuggable, and it is the most common instrumentation gap.<br><br><strong>What observability enables that metrics cannot:</strong> replaying a failure with a modified prompt; converting any production failure into an eval case in one click; comparing behavior across model versions on identical inputs; and root-causing a quality complaint to the specific retrieval or reasoning step that went wrong.<br><br><strong>Tooling:</strong> LangSmith, Langfuse, Phoenix, Braintrust, and OpenTelemetry's GenAI semantic conventions for vendor-neutral instrumentation.<br><br><strong>The constraint to design for:</strong> traces contain user data, so retention, redaction, and access control are part of the design — not an afterthought. PII in traces is a common and avoidable compliance failure.` },

{ q: 'What are guardrails for LLMs, and how do you implement them?', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `Guardrails are enforcement mechanisms <em>outside</em> the model that constrain inputs, outputs, and actions. The defining principle: <strong>anything enforceable in code must be enforced in code.</strong> Instructing the model is a preference, not a control.<br><br><strong>Input guardrails:</strong> prompt-injection classifiers (on user input <em>and</em> on retrieved/tool content), PII and secret detection, topic/scope classifiers rejecting off-mandate requests, and length limits.<br><br><strong>Output guardrails:</strong> schema validation for structured output, content moderation, PII redaction, groundedness checks against provided sources for RAG, and blocking exfiltration patterns — model-generated URLs and image requests to non-allowlisted domains are a common data-leak channel.<br><br><strong>Action guardrails (for agents — the most important layer):</strong> tool allowlisting, argument validation in code, authorization checked against the <em>authenticated user's</em> permissions rather than the model's stated intent, human approval for irreversible actions, and rate limits per tool.<br><br><strong>Implementation options:</strong> deterministic checks (regex, schema, business rules) are the cheapest and most reliable — use them first. Small classifiers for moderation and injection detection. LLM judges only for genuinely nuanced policy checks, where cost and latency are justified.<br><br><strong>Design the blast radius:</strong> assume every probabilistic guardrail fails and ask what the worst outcome is. If unacceptable, the fix is fewer permissions or a mandatory human gate.<br><br><strong>Instrument them:</strong> log every trigger with the full trace. A rising block rate is your earliest signal of an attack or a prompt regression.` },

{ q: 'How do you implement content filtering for AI outputs?', tags: [], a: `<strong>Layer it, cheapest first:</strong> deterministic checks (blocklists, regex for PII patterns, URL allowlists) → a fast moderation classifier → an LLM judge only for genuinely ambiguous cases. Running an LLM on every output is unaffordable at volume and unnecessary, since the vast majority is clearly fine.<br><br><strong>Use the provider's moderation endpoint</strong> where available — it is cheap, fast, and maintained. Supplement with your own domain policy, since generic moderation will not know your product's specific prohibitions.<br><br><strong>The streaming problem is the interesting engineering constraint.</strong> If you stream tokens to the user, you cannot moderate the complete response before it is seen. Options: buffer sentence-by-sentence and moderate each chunk before release (adds latency, but bounded); stream optimistically and retract on violation (bad UX, and the user may have already read it); or moderate a fast draft before streaming. Most products buffer at sentence granularity as the pragmatic compromise — decide deliberately rather than discovering it late.<br><br><strong>Set the operating point per category.</strong> Self-harm and CSAM: maximize recall, accept false positives. Mild profanity: favor precision, since over-blocking frustrates users. A single global threshold across categories is the most common design error.<br><br><strong>Handle blocks gracefully:</strong> a clear message beats a silent failure or a generic error, and log the block with the trace so false positives are reviewable.<br><br><strong>Measure both directions</strong> — false-positive rate on benign content matters as much as catch rate, and only the first one is visible in user complaints.` },

{ q: 'How do you estimate the cost of an AI feature in production?', tags: [], a: `<strong>Build the unit-economics model first:</strong> cost per request = (input tokens × input price) + (output tokens × output price). Then multiply by expected volume. The mistake teams make is estimating from a single example rather than from the <em>distribution</em>.<br><br><strong>Estimate token counts empirically.</strong> Run realistic examples through a tokenizer and measure — do not guess from word counts. For RAG, input tokens are dominated by retrieved context (k × chunk size), which is usually far larger than the user's query and is the term to attack first.<br><br><strong>Model the distribution, not the mean.</strong> Cost per request is heavily long-tailed: agent trajectories, long documents, and multi-turn conversations produce outliers orders of magnitude above median. Budget on p95, and cap max context and max output as hard rails so the tail is bounded.<br><br><strong>Include the components people forget:</strong> embedding costs for ingestion (a one-time but potentially large batch cost) and for every query; reranking; retries and fallbacks; guardrail model calls; and evaluation runs, which for an actively developed system can be a meaningful fraction of total spend.<br><br><strong>Apply the discounts:</strong> prompt caching can cut input cost by up to ~90% on the cacheable prefix, and batch APIs typically offer ~50% for latency-tolerant work. These change the answer materially, so model them explicitly.<br><br><strong>Then sanity-check against value:</strong> cost per request versus revenue or cost saved per request. A feature costing $0.30/request in a workflow that saves 20 minutes is excellent; the same cost on a free-tier chat feature is not.` },

{ q: 'How do you optimize LLM inference costs in production?', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `<strong>Measure first</strong> — split spend into input versus output tokens and attribute by feature. Output tokens typically cost 3–5x input, but input volume is usually far larger in RAG systems, so the dominant term varies and guessing wastes effort.<br><br><strong>Highest-leverage changes, roughly in order:</strong><br>• <strong>Prompt caching</strong> — reorder prompts so static content (system prompt, tools, examples) comes first and variable content last. Up to ~90% input cost reduction with zero quality tradeoff. Usually the single best change available.<br>• <strong>Model routing</strong> — classify difficulty and send the easy majority to a small model, escalating only the hard tail. Most production traffic is easy, so a uniform frontier model over-serves it.<br>• <strong>Reduce input tokens</strong> — rerank so you can drop from k=20 to k=5 chunks. This cuts cost <em>and</em> improves quality by avoiding lost-in-the-middle.<br>• <strong>Reduce output tokens</strong> — explicit length budgets, structured fields instead of prose, and skipping chain-of-thought where it does not help.<br>• <strong>Caching</strong> — exact-match always; semantic caching with a conservative threshold for FAQ-like traffic.<br>• <strong>Batch API</strong> for anything latency-tolerant, typically ~50% off.<br><br><strong>Structural options:</strong> fine-tune a small model to replace a large one on a narrow task — often 10–20x cheaper at equal quality, and the strongest long-term lever.<br><br><strong>The discipline that matters:</strong> run every change against your eval set. Cost optimization without a quality gate reliably ships something cheaper and worse.` },

{ q: 'How do you implement A/B testing for LLM systems?', tags: ['TRADEOFF'], a: `<strong>Structurally the same as any A/B test — but three things make it harder.</strong><br><br><strong>1. Output is non-deterministic,</strong> so per-request variance is high and you need larger samples to detect the same effect. Pre-compute the sample size for your expected effect rather than peeking until significance appears.<br><br><strong>2. Quality is hard to measure online.</strong> You rarely have ground truth in production, so rely on proxy signals: thumbs up/down, regeneration rate, conversation abandonment, escalation to human, task completion, and downstream business metrics. Regeneration rate is underrated — a strong implicit quality signal that costs nothing.<br><br><strong>3. Assignment must be sticky per user or session.</strong> Flipping a user between prompt variants mid-conversation produces incoherent behavior and contaminates the measurement.<br><br><strong>Run offline evaluation first.</strong> A/B tests are expensive in time and traffic; use your eval set to filter candidates down to those worth testing live. Shipping every idea to an A/B test is a slow way to learn.<br><br><strong>Measure cost and latency alongside quality</strong> — a variant that wins on quality at 3x cost is usually a loss, and reporting quality alone hides that.<br><br><strong>Use shadow mode for risky changes:</strong> run the variant in parallel without serving it, log both outputs, and compare offline with an LLM judge or human review. Zero user risk, and it catches catastrophic regressions before any traffic sees them.<br><br><strong>Watch for interaction effects:</strong> a prompt change can shift retrieval behavior or tool usage in ways that only appear in aggregate metrics, not in per-response quality.` },

{ q: 'What is CI/CD for AI applications, and how does it differ?', tags: [], a: `<strong>The pipeline shape is familiar</strong> — lint, test, build, deploy — but three things are genuinely different.<br><br><strong>1. Tests are statistical, not deterministic.</strong> You cannot assert exact output. Instead run an eval set and gate on aggregate metrics with a threshold, ideally over multiple runs to account for variance. A test that asserts an exact string will flake constantly and get disabled.<br><br><strong>2. Prompts and retrieval configs are deployable artifacts</strong> that need the same treatment as code: version control, code review, and a regression eval on every change. Teams that store prompts in a database "for fast iteration" end up with no review, no history, and no way to explain a quality drop.<br><br><strong>3. Your dependency can change without a deploy on your side.</strong> A provider updating a model endpoint changes behavior with no commit in your repo. That means scheduled evaluation runs against production config, not just on-commit — and explicit model version pinning wherever the provider supports it.<br><br><strong>What to gate on in CI:</strong> eval-set metrics against baseline, cost per request (catch a change that triples spend), latency, schema-validation rate, and safety/guardrail tests. Fast deterministic checks run per-commit; full eval suites can run per-PR or nightly if they are slow.<br><br><strong>Deployment:</strong> shadow → canary → progressive rollout, with automatic rollback on metric regression. Never a full cutover, because behavior is statistical and the failure mode is silent degradation rather than a crash.` },

{ q: 'How do you version and manage prompts in production?', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `<strong>Treat prompts as code.</strong> Store them in the repository, version with git, review via pull requests, and deploy with the application. The alternative — prompts in a database edited through an admin UI — gives you fast iteration and no review, no history, no attribution, and no way to correlate a quality drop with a change.<br><br><strong>What to version together:</strong> the prompt template, model name and version, sampling parameters, tool definitions, and retrieval config. These interact — a prompt tuned for one model behaves differently on another — so versioning the prompt alone is insufficient. Bundle them as one configuration artifact with a single version id.<br><br><strong>Log the version with every request,</strong> and log the <em>rendered</em> prompt (or its hash), not just the template. This is what makes production failures reproducible; without it you cannot tell whether a bad output came from the prompt, an empty variable, or truncated context.<br><br><strong>Deployment:</strong> gate every prompt change on an eval-set run in CI, then canary. A prompt change is a behavior change and deserves the same caution as a code change — it is not a config tweak.<br><br><strong>Rollback must be instant:</strong> keep the previous version deployable and switchable via feature flag, since prompt regressions are often only visible in production traffic patterns.<br><br><strong>The middle ground</strong> if non-engineers need to iterate: let them experiment in a playground against the eval set, but require the change to land through the normal review pipeline. Fast iteration belongs in the eval loop, not in production mutation.` },

{ q: 'What is model versioning, and how do you handle rollbacks?', tags: [], a: `<strong>Pin model versions explicitly.</strong> Using a floating alias means the provider can change your model — and therefore your product's behavior — with no deploy on your side. Pin to a dated snapshot wherever the provider offers one, and track deprecation timelines so you migrate deliberately rather than being forced.<br><br><strong>Version the whole configuration together:</strong> model version, prompt, parameters, tool definitions, and retrieval config. These interact, so a rollback must restore all of them atomically. Rolling back the model but keeping the new prompt frequently produces a state neither version was tested in.<br><br><strong>Deployment pattern:</strong> shadow (run in parallel, log, do not serve) → canary at low traffic → progressive rollout, with automatic rollback triggered on metric regression. The metrics worth auto-rolling-back on are error rate, schema-validation failure rate, latency, and cost — all fast, unambiguous signals. Quality regressions are slower to detect, so keep the old version deployable for longer than feels necessary.<br><br><strong>The self-hosted case adds a wrinkle:</strong> rollback means reloading tens of GB of weights, which is minutes. Keep the previous version warm on standby capacity if fast rollback matters, or accept the recovery time explicitly in your SLO.<br><br><strong>What makes rollback actually work:</strong> statelessness. If a model version change altered stored data — embeddings, extracted fields, cached responses — you cannot roll back by switching a flag. Embeddings are the classic case: a model change requires a full re-index, so plan it as a blue/green migration, not a deploy.` },

{ q: 'How do you implement rate limiting for LLM APIs?', tags: [], a: `<strong>Limit on tokens, not just requests.</strong> This is the key difference from conventional APIs: a single request can be 100 tokens or 100k, so requests-per-minute bounds neither cost nor capacity. Enforce tokens-per-minute alongside requests-per-minute, and count <em>estimated</em> output tokens at admission rather than only actuals after completion — otherwise you admit work you cannot afford.<br><br><strong>Add a concurrency cap,</strong> which is what actually protects GPU capacity. One user with 200 in-flight long generations can saturate a pool while staying comfortably under an RPM limit. Concurrency is the limit that maps to hardware.<br><br><strong>Layer the limits:</strong> per-user, per-organization, per-API-key, and a global ceiling. Token bucket smooths bursts while bounding sustained rate.<br><br><strong>Degrade rather than reject where possible:</strong> queue with backpressure and honest <code>Retry-After</code> headers, so clients can back off intelligently instead of hammering. Return 429 with a clear distinction between "you are rate limited" and "the service is down" — clients should retry the first and fail over on the second.<br><br><strong>Handle the upstream case too:</strong> you are also a client of a provider with its own limits. Implement client-side rate limiting, exponential backoff with jitter, and a request queue so provider 429s do not cascade into user-facing errors.<br><br><strong>Tiering is the product decision:</strong> different limits per plan, with the ability to burst above sustained rate. And monitor rejection rate — a high rate means your limits are miscalibrated or a customer needs a conversation, not silent throttling.` },

{ q: 'How do you handle model updates and migrations without downtime?', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `<strong>Standard blue/green with an AI-specific twist: the new version must be validated on <em>quality</em>, not just health checks.</strong> A model that responds fast and returns 200s can still be much worse, and no conventional readiness probe detects that.<br><br><strong>The sequence:</strong><br>1. <em>Shadow</em> — route a copy of production traffic to the new version without serving its responses. Log both, compare offline with an LLM judge or human review. Zero user risk, and it catches catastrophic regressions before any traffic sees them.<br>2. <em>Canary</em> — 1–5% of traffic, monitoring error rate, latency, cost, schema-validation rate, and user signals like regeneration rate.<br>3. <em>Progressive rollout</em> with automatic rollback on metric regression.<br>4. Keep the old version deployable for longer than feels necessary, since quality regressions surface slowly.<br><br><strong>The migration that is <em>not</em> zero-downtime-able by flag flip: embeddings.</strong> Changing an embedding model requires re-embedding the entire corpus, and vectors from two models cannot coexist in one index. That is a parallel-index build with dual-writes during the transition, validation against a labeled retrieval eval set, then an atomic read cutover — a project, not a deploy.<br><br><strong>For self-hosted models:</strong> GPU cold starts mean you must bring up new capacity before draining the old, so plan for temporarily doubled capacity during the transition.<br><br><strong>Test prompts against the new model before migrating.</strong> Prompt behavior does not transfer cleanly across versions, and "the model upgrade broke our formatting" is an extremely common incident.` },

{ q: 'What is the role of feature flags in AI deployments?', tags: [], a: `Feature flags decouple deployment from release, which matters more for AI than for conventional software because <strong>you often cannot know if a change is good until real traffic sees it</strong>.<br><br><strong>What to put behind flags:</strong> model version, prompt version, retrieval configuration (k, reranking on/off, chunking strategy), which features are enabled per tenant, guardrail strictness, and the fallback/degradation level.<br><br><strong>The high-value uses:</strong><br>• <em>Instant rollback</em> without a deploy — critical when a prompt regression is only visible in production and you need to revert in seconds, not through a CI pipeline.<br>• <em>Progressive rollout</em> — 1% → 10% → 50% → 100%, with automatic rollback on metric regression.<br>• <em>A/B testing</em> — sticky per-user assignment between variants.<br>• <em>Manual degradation during incidents</em> — force a cheaper model or disable an expensive feature to shed load, which is far faster than deploying a change under pressure.<br>• <em>Per-tenant configuration</em> in multi-tenant products, where one customer needs different behavior.<br><br><strong>The discipline that keeps this from rotting:</strong> flags accumulate, and stale flags create untested code paths and combinatorial state. Set expiry dates and remove them once a rollout completes.<br><br><strong>Second-order concern specific to AI:</strong> flag combinations multiply the configurations you are actually serving. If model version, prompt version, and retrieval config are independently flagged, your eval coverage should include the combinations you can actually be in — not just the default path.` },

{ q: 'How do you implement logging and tracing for LLM applications?', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `<strong>Trace the whole request as a tree of spans:</strong> query rewriting → retrieval (chunk ids, scores, filters applied) → reranking → prompt assembly → model call → tool calls → output → guardrail checks. Each span carries latency, tokens, and cost.<br><br><strong>Log the <em>rendered</em> prompt, not the template.</strong> This is the single most important instrumentation decision. Most "the model ignored my instruction" bugs turn out to be rendering bugs — an empty variable, a chunk that truncated the tail of the prompt, a malformed chat template. Without the literal string sent to the API, those are undebuggable, and this is the most common gap I see.<br><br><strong>Also capture:</strong> model name and version, all sampling parameters, retrieved chunk ids with scores (so you can distinguish a retrieval failure from a generation failure), tool call arguments and results, and the prompt/config version id.<br><br><strong>Use OpenTelemetry's GenAI semantic conventions</strong> for vendor-neutral instrumentation, or a purpose-built platform (LangSmith, Langfuse, Phoenix, Braintrust) that gives you trace visualization, dataset curation from traces, and eval integration.<br><br><strong>The payoff is the feedback loop:</strong> a production failure becomes an eval case in one click, and you can replay it against a modified prompt. That loop is the actual development process for LLM systems.<br><br><strong>The constraint to design in from the start:</strong> traces contain user data. Redaction, retention limits, and access control are requirements, not follow-ups — PII sitting in a trace store is a common and avoidable compliance failure.` },

{ q: 'How do you handle PII and sensitive data in LLM inputs and outputs?', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `<strong>Start with data flow mapping:</strong> what data reaches the model, where it goes (provider, region), what is retained (by you and by the provider), and who can access logs and traces. Most PII incidents are architectural — data going somewhere nobody intended — rather than detection failures.<br><br><strong>Minimize first.</strong> The cheapest control is not sending PII at all: pass an account id rather than a name and address, retrieve only the fields needed, and strip attachments and metadata. This eliminates whole categories of risk rather than mitigating them.<br><br><strong>Then redact or tokenize.</strong> Detect PII (Presidio, cloud DLP services, or a fine-tuned NER model) and replace with placeholders before the model call, re-hydrating in your own layer afterward. This preserves utility while keeping raw identifiers out of third-party systems — though note detection is imperfect, so treat it as defense in depth, not a guarantee.<br><br><strong>On the output side:</strong> scan generations for PII leakage, particularly in RAG systems where retrieved context may contain data the requesting user should not see. Access control at retrieval is the real fix; output scanning is the backstop.<br><br><strong>Contractual and architectural controls:</strong> zero-retention endpoints where providers offer them, BAAs for healthcare, regional endpoints for residency, and no-training-on-customer-data terms confirmed in writing.<br><br><strong>The point worth making explicitly:</strong> never put PII in training data. Weights cannot honor a deletion request — machine unlearning is a research problem, not a production tool. Anything that might need to be deleted, revoked, or access-controlled belongs in a retrieval store. That is a compliance argument for RAG, not just a freshness one.<br><br><strong>And redact traces</strong> — logs are the most commonly overlooked PII repository.` },

{ q: 'What is a gateway pattern for LLM API management?', tags: [], a: `An LLM gateway is a proxy every model call routes through, centralizing cross-cutting concerns that every team would otherwise reimplement — usually badly and inconsistently.<br><br><strong>What it owns:</strong> authentication and per-team key mapping; cost attribution by team, feature, and user; token-based rate limiting and budget enforcement; a unified interface across providers (which is what makes failover possible at all); routing by model tier or cost; caching; centralized guardrails (PII redaction, injection detection, content filtering); and full request/response logging with traces.<br><br><strong>The strongest argument is governance.</strong> Security and compliance can answer "what data went to which provider" only if there is one path. Without a gateway, that question is unanswerable in most organizations, and policy application depends on each team remembering.<br><br><strong>The second-strongest is provider portability:</strong> abstracting the interface is what lets you fail over during an outage or migrate when pricing changes. Teams calling provider SDKs directly are locked in by a hundred call sites.<br><br><strong>The risks to design against:</strong><br>• It becomes a single point of failure on every AI feature, so it needs higher availability than anything behind it.<br>• It must add minimal latency — stream pass-through rather than buffering, or you destroy TTFT.<br>• If it is slow or restrictive, teams route around it and you lose the governance benefit entirely.<br><br><strong>Options:</strong> LiteLLM, Portkey, Kong AI Gateway, or a thin in-house service. Build only if your requirements are genuinely unusual — this is well-served by existing tools.` },

{ q: 'How does token streaming work?', tags: [], a: `Streaming sends each generated token to the client as it is produced rather than waiting for the full response. Transport is typically Server-Sent Events (simple, HTTP-native, one-directional) or WebSockets when you need bidirectional communication.<br><br><strong>Why it matters:</strong> it changes the latency the user <em>experiences</em> from total generation time to time-to-first-token. A 3-second response that starts appearing in 300ms feels immediate; the same response delivered whole after 3 seconds feels slow. For long generations this is the difference between usable and unusable, and it is usually the highest-ROI latency work available because it requires no quality tradeoff.<br><br><strong>Implementation details that bite:</strong><br>• <em>Buffering breaks it.</em> Proxies, load balancers, and frameworks that buffer responses silently destroy streaming — you must disable buffering end to end, and this is the most common cause of "streaming doesn't work in production but works locally."<br>• <em>Errors mid-stream</em> arrive after a 200 status, so you need in-band error signaling; the HTTP status is already sent.<br>• <em>Client disconnects</em> should cancel generation upstream, or you keep paying for tokens nobody receives.<br>• <em>Token boundaries</em> are not character boundaries — multi-byte UTF-8 can split across tokens, so accumulate before decoding.<br><br><strong>The conflict with output validation:</strong> you cannot validate a schema or moderate content you have not finished generating. Common resolutions are buffering at sentence granularity for moderation, or streaming only for free-form text and buffering structured output.` },

{ q: 'How do you implement streaming responses for real-time applications?', tags: [], a: `<strong>Server side:</strong> use the provider's streaming API, forward chunks as SSE events, and ensure nothing in the path buffers — application framework, reverse proxy, CDN, and load balancer all need explicit configuration. Buffering somewhere in the chain is the most common reason streaming works locally and not in production.<br><br><strong>Client side:</strong> render incrementally, handle reconnection, and give the user a stop control — which must actually cancel the upstream generation, not just hide the output, or you keep paying for tokens.<br><br><strong>The design tensions to resolve deliberately:</strong><br>• <em>Validation.</em> You cannot schema-validate or moderate a response you have not finished generating. Resolutions: buffer at sentence granularity and moderate each chunk (bounded added latency), or stream free-form text and buffer structured output entirely.<br>• <em>Error handling.</em> A failure mid-stream arrives after a 200 status, so you need in-band error events and a client that can render a partial response plus an error state.<br>• <em>Retries.</em> A stream that fails halfway cannot be transparently retried without either duplicating or losing content — decide whether you restart or surface the partial.<br><br><strong>For agents, stream more than tokens:</strong> emit intermediate progress events ("searching…", "found 12 results, analyzing"). A 30-second agent task with visible progress is acceptable; the same task with a spinner feels broken. This is frequently the highest-value UX change in agentic products.<br><br><strong>Measure TTFT separately from total latency</strong> — it is the metric users actually feel, and optimizing total time while TTFT regresses is a net loss.` },

{ q: 'How does vLLM work?', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `vLLM is a high-throughput inference server whose central contribution is <strong>PagedAttention</strong>.<br><br><strong>The problem it solves:</strong> naive serving pre-allocates a contiguous KV cache block per sequence sized for the maximum possible length. Since most sequences are far shorter, 60–80% of allocated cache is wasted on internal fragmentation — which directly caps how many concurrent requests fit in GPU memory, and concurrency is what determines throughput.<br><br><strong>PagedAttention</strong> borrows OS virtual memory: store the KV cache in fixed-size blocks that need not be contiguous, with a block table mapping logical positions to physical blocks. Memory waste drops to under ~4%. It also enables <em>copy-on-write sharing</em> — multiple requests with a common system prompt prefix, or parallel samples from one prompt, physically share those blocks rather than duplicating them.<br><br><strong>Continuous batching</strong> is the second pillar: rather than waiting for every sequence in a batch to finish, vLLM admits new requests and evicts completed ones at each decode step, keeping the GPU saturated under variable-length generation. Combined, these deliver roughly an order of magnitude more throughput than naive HuggingFace generation.<br><br><strong>What else it provides:</strong> chunked prefill (interleaving long prefills with decode so TTFT spikes do not stall other users), multi-LoRA serving with per-request adapter selection, quantization support, speculative decoding, tensor parallelism, and an OpenAI-compatible API.<br><br><strong>The practical takeaway:</strong> the gap between naive serving and vLLM is large enough that self-hosting without it is usually a mistake — you are paying for many times the GPUs you need.` },

{ q: 'How does SGLang work?', tags: [], a: `SGLang is an inference framework whose distinguishing contribution is <strong>RadixAttention</strong> — automatic, cross-request KV cache reuse organized as a radix tree over token prefixes.<br><br><strong>The insight:</strong> vLLM's prefix sharing handles explicit cases (identical system prompts, parallel samples). RadixAttention generalizes it: the cache is a prefix tree, so <em>any</em> shared prefix between <em>any</em> requests is automatically reused, with LRU eviction. That is a large win for workloads with heavy prefix overlap — multi-turn conversations (each turn shares the entire prior conversation), few-shot prompting, agent loops where each step re-sends the accumulated trajectory, and tree-search patterns.<br><br><strong>Agent workloads benefit most</strong>, because a ReAct loop resends the whole growing history at every step — exactly the pattern RadixAttention is designed for.<br><br><strong>The second contribution is the frontend DSL:</strong> a Python-embedded language for expressing generation programs — branching, parallelism, constrained decoding, multi-call structures — that the runtime can then optimize as a whole, including scheduling calls to maximize cache reuse. Co-designing the program representation with the runtime is the architectural idea worth taking away.<br><br><strong>Also:</strong> fast constrained decoding via compressed finite state machines, structured output support, and standard features (continuous batching, tensor parallelism, quantization).<br><br><strong>How to choose:</strong> vLLM is the more mature, broadly adopted default. SGLang is compelling when your workload has heavy prefix sharing or complex multi-call structure — benchmark both on your actual traffic pattern rather than on published numbers.` },

{ q: 'What are the key SLAs and metrics for production AI systems?', tags: [], a: `<strong>Latency, split into two metrics</strong> — this is the AI-specific part. <em>Time-to-first-token</em> (TTFT) is what users perceive as responsiveness; typical target p95 under 500ms–1s for interactive use. <em>Inter-token latency</em> (or tokens/sec) determines whether output renders faster than reading speed; ~50ms per token is comfortable. Reporting only total latency hides which half is broken.<br><br><strong>Throughput:</strong> requests/sec and tokens/sec per GPU. But the metric that actually matters is <strong>goodput</strong> — requests completed <em>within SLO</em>. A system serving high raw throughput while missing latency targets is not delivering, and only goodput captures that.<br><br><strong>Availability:</strong> define it as successful, on-SLO responses rather than endpoint uptime. A service returning 200s with degraded quality is not available in any meaningful sense.<br><br><strong>Cost:</strong> cost per request and per feature, tracked as a distribution rather than a mean — it is heavily long-tailed and the tail drives the bill.<br><br><strong>Quality metrics, which are the ones teams omit:</strong> sampled judge scores, groundedness/citation validity, schema-validation failure rate, guardrail trigger rate, and refusal rate. Schema failure rate is an excellent leading indicator — it spikes immediately on a model or prompt change.<br><br><strong>User signals:</strong> thumbs down rate, regeneration rate, escalation rate.<br><br><strong>The framing to state:</strong> conventional SLOs assume failures are loud. LLM systems fail quietly — right status code, wrong answer — so quality metrics are not optional extras; they are the availability signal.` },

{ q: 'Cloud vs on-device model deployment.', tags: ['TRADEOFF'], a: `<strong>Cloud:</strong> frontier capability, no device constraints, instant model updates, centralized monitoring and iteration. Costs: per-request pricing that scales with usage, network latency and dependency, data leaves the device (a hard blocker in some regulated or privacy-sensitive contexts), and no offline capability.<br><br><strong>On-device:</strong> data never leaves the device, zero marginal inference cost, no network latency, works offline. Costs: much lower capability (1–4B quantized models versus frontier), device fragmentation requiring graceful degradation on older hardware, model updates gated on app releases, large binary size, and battery/thermal limits on sustained inference.<br><br><strong>The framing that matters: it is rarely either/or.</strong> The right architecture for most products is <em>hybrid routing</em> — an on-device classifier decides per request whether local suffices. Local handles high-frequency, latency-sensitive, privacy-sensitive work (intent classification, summarizing local content, simple Q&A, on-device data lookup); cloud handles complex reasoning and broad world knowledge.<br><br><strong>That gives you the properties that actually sell:</strong> privacy and instant response on the common path, frontier quality on the tail, and graceful offline behavior.<br><br><strong>How I'd decide the split:</strong> profile your query distribution. If 80% of requests are simple classification or extraction, on-device handles them at zero marginal cost and sub-100ms latency — a strong win. If your workload is uniformly complex reasoning, on-device adds engineering burden for little benefit.<br><br><strong>The constraint that often decides it outright:</strong> regulatory data-residency or offline requirements make on-device mandatory regardless of the capability tradeoff.` },

{ q: 'How do you implement fallback strategies when the primary model is unavailable?', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `<strong>Build a ladder that degrades rather than fails:</strong><br>1. Retry with exponential backoff and jitter (handles transient errors and rate limits).<br>2. Failover to a different region/deployment of the same model.<br>3. Failover to a different provider.<br>4. Fall back to a smaller or self-hosted model.<br>5. Serve a cached or precomputed response.<br>6. Degrade the feature — return retrieval results without generation, which is genuinely useful and has an independent failure domain.<br>7. Fail honestly with a clear message.<br><br><strong>The step teams skip is validating the fallback.</strong> A prompt tuned for one model often behaves poorly on another, so an untested fallback silently ships bad output during exactly the incident where you cannot investigate. Run your eval set against every fallback model and keep it in CI — otherwise you have a failover path, not a working one.<br><br><strong>Use circuit breakers, not just retries.</strong> When a provider is degraded, aggressive retries make it worse and consume your latency budget. Trip after a failure threshold, route to the fallback, probe periodically for recovery.<br><br><strong>Distinguish error types</strong> — they need different responses: rate limits (backoff and retry), 5xx (failover), content-filter blocks (do not retry — it will block again), timeouts (may have partially succeeded, so consider idempotency).<br><br><strong>Prerequisite:</strong> abstract model calls behind a common interface. Teams calling provider SDKs directly at a hundred call sites cannot fail over at all.<br><br><strong>Test it with failure injection regularly</strong> — a path first exercised during an outage usually does not work.` },

{ q: 'How do you implement structured output reliably in production?', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `<strong>Escalate by mechanism strength, not by rewriting prompts.</strong><br><br><strong>1. Constrained decoding</strong> — the only hard guarantee. Mask logits at each step so only tokens valid under your JSON Schema or grammar can be sampled (provider structured-output modes, or outlines/llguidance/XGrammar with vLLM). Invalid output becomes structurally impossible rather than merely discouraged.<br><br><strong>2. Native tool/function calling</strong> — models are specifically fine-tuned for it, so adherence far exceeds free-form prompting.<br><br><strong>3. Prompt-level techniques</strong> when neither is available: give a real schema rather than prose, place it near the end for recency, provide one or two exact-format examples, temperature 0, prefill the assistant turn with <code>{</code>, and use a stop sequence to cut trailing commentary.<br><br><strong>4. Always validate at the boundary</strong> with Pydantic or zod, and on failure retry once with the parse error appended so the model can repair. That single repair loop resolves most residual failures.<br><br><strong>The tradeoff to state:</strong> tight structural constraint can degrade content quality, because format compliance competes with reasoning. If you observe that, split it — let the model reason freely in one call, then run a cheap constrained extraction over that output.<br><br><strong>Schema design matters as much as the mechanism:</strong> flat schemas with few fields and enums for closed value sets have markedly better adherence than deeply nested structures with many optionals. Design the schema for the model, not for your database — transform afterward.<br><br><strong>Monitor schema-failure rate</strong> as a first-class metric; it is the earliest signal of a model or prompt regression.` },

{ q: 'How do you handle long contexts efficiently in production?', tags: ['TRADEOFF'], a: `<strong>First question the premise: more context is usually the wrong fix.</strong> Cost and latency scale with tokens, and "lost in the middle" means a 100k-token prompt can retrieve <em>worse</em> than a well-targeted 4k one. Measure before buying context.<br><br><strong>Reduce what goes in:</strong><br>• <em>Retrieval instead of stuffing</em> — for selective tasks (the answer lives in a few passages), retrieve and rerank rather than passing the whole document. Cheaper and typically more accurate.<br>• <em>Rerank and cut k</em> — five strong chunks beat fifty mediocre ones, saving cost and improving quality simultaneously.<br>• <em>Context compression</em> — extract only relevant sentences from each chunk before insertion, or use an LLM to condense.<br>• <em>Hierarchical processing</em> — map-reduce for holistic tasks over long documents, parallelizable and bounded per call.<br><br><strong>Reduce what it costs:</strong><br>• <em>Prefix caching</em> — order prompts static-first so the cacheable prefix is maximal. Up to ~90% input cost reduction with no quality tradeoff.<br>• <em>KV cache quantization</em> (fp8/int8) for self-hosted serving, cutting memory 2–4x.<br>• <em>GQA models</em>, which shrink the KV cache 4–8x architecturally.<br><br><strong>Order deliberately:</strong> place the highest-value content at the start and end, and restate the question after the context — this measurably counteracts the U-shaped attention curve.<br><br><strong>Verify on your own stack:</strong> run a needle-in-a-haystack test at your real context lengths and model. Severity varies a lot by model, and advertised context length says nothing about usable retrieval quality across it.` },

{ q: 'What is semantic routing, and how do you implement it?', tags: [], a: `Semantic routing classifies an incoming request by meaning and dispatches it to the appropriate handler — a specific model, prompt, tool set, or knowledge base.<br><br><strong>Why it matters:</strong> most production traffic is easy, and serving it with a frontier model over-provisions the common case. Routing lets you spend proportionally to difficulty, which is typically the largest cost lever available after prompt caching. It also improves quality, since a specialized prompt and scoped tool set outperform one general-purpose configuration.<br><br><strong>Implementation options, cheapest first:</strong><br>• <em>Embedding similarity</em> — embed the query and match against centroids of labeled example queries per route. Sub-10ms, essentially free, and surprisingly effective for well-separated intents.<br>• <em>Fine-tuned classifier</em> — a small encoder model. Fast, cheap, accurate, and the right answer when you have labeled data.<br>• <em>LLM classifier</em> — flexible and handles nuance, but adds a full model call to every request, which partly defeats the cost purpose. Use for genuinely ambiguous routing.<br><br><strong>Design requirements:</strong> a default route for anything unmatched (never fail closed on an unrecognized query), a confidence threshold below which you escalate to the stronger path, and separate measurement of routing accuracy — a routing error is invisible in end-to-end metrics but shows up as unexplained quality variance.<br><br><strong>The failure to design against:</strong> misrouting a hard query to the cheap model produces a confidently poor answer with no signal. Bias the threshold toward escalation, and monitor per-route quality rather than aggregate.` },

{ q: 'How do you manage secrets and API keys securely in LLM applications?', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `<strong>The standard practices apply</strong> — secrets in a manager (Vault, AWS Secrets Manager, cloud KMS), never in code or environment files committed to the repo, rotated regularly, scoped to least privilege, and never logged.<br><br><strong>What is AI-specific and frequently missed:</strong><br><br><strong>1. Never put secrets in a prompt or system prompt.</strong> Assume the system prompt is extractable — every published prompt-protection technique has been broken. A key in a system prompt should be treated as already public.<br><br><strong>2. Agents must never hold credentials they can be talked into using.</strong> The tool layer holds the credential and enforces authorization against the <em>authenticated user's</em> permissions; the model only expresses intent. If the model can pass a credential as a tool argument, prompt injection becomes credential theft.<br><br><strong>3. Traces and logs are a major leak surface.</strong> Prompts, tool arguments, and retrieved context all get logged, and any of them can contain secrets — an API key pasted by a user, a connection string in a retrieved document. Redact at the logging layer, not just at the application layer.<br><br><strong>4. Scope provider keys per environment and per team,</strong> so a leaked development key cannot spend production budget, and cost attribution works. Use per-team keys through a gateway rather than one shared organizational key.<br><br><strong>5. Model outputs can contain memorized secrets</strong> if training or retrieval data contained them — scan outputs for credential patterns.<br><br><strong>Monitor for anomalous usage</strong> — a leaked LLM API key is exploited for compute, and unusual spend is often the first signal.` },

{ q: 'Your LLM API has latency spikes at peak hours. How do you stabilize it?', tags: ['TRADEOFF'], a: `<strong>Diagnose the source first</strong> — split latency into queue wait, TTFT, and generation time, and correlate with concurrency. The fix differs completely depending on which component spikes, and guessing wastes a cycle.<br><br><strong>If it is queueing (most common at peak):</strong> add admission control. An unbounded queue converts a capacity problem into a latency catastrophe where everyone waits. Reject or degrade above a threshold with an honest <code>Retry-After</code>, and use priority queues so interactive traffic is not stuck behind batch work.<br><br><strong>If it is provider-side (for API-based systems):</strong> you cannot fix their capacity, so route around it — multi-provider failover, provisioned throughput/reserved capacity where offered, and client-side rate limiting so you degrade predictably rather than getting throttled unpredictably.<br><br><strong>If it is TTFT:</strong> prefill is the cost, so shorten prompts (rerank and cut k) and use prompt caching, which cuts both cost and TTFT on the cached prefix.<br><br><strong>Structural changes that flatten the peak:</strong> move latency-tolerant work to a batch API or off-peak queue — a surprising amount of "real-time" workload is not; cache aggressively, since peak traffic often has high query overlap; and route easy queries to a smaller, faster model during peak specifically.<br><br><strong>For self-hosted:</strong> pre-scale on schedule rather than reactively — GPU cold starts are minutes, so reactive autoscaling arrives after the spike is over.<br><br><strong>Set the SLO honestly:</strong> if peak capacity is 3x average, you either pay for 3x provisioning or accept degradation. Make that an explicit product decision rather than an emergent one.` },

{ q: 'Your LLM costs are too high. How do you reduce them without degrading quality?', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `<strong>Attribute first.</strong> Split spend by feature, by input versus output tokens, and look at the <em>distribution</em>. Cost is heavily long-tailed — a small number of requests (long agent trajectories, huge contexts, retry storms) usually drives most of the bill, and capping that tail is often the biggest single win.<br><br><strong>The changes with no quality cost, do these first:</strong><br>• <strong>Prompt caching</strong> — reorder prompts static-first. Up to ~90% input cost reduction, zero quality tradeoff, and it also improves TTFT. Almost always the best available change.<br>• <strong>Exact-match response caching</strong> for repeated queries.<br>• <strong>Batch API</strong> (~50% discount) for anything latency-tolerant — evaluation runs, bulk processing, offline enrichment.<br>• <strong>Cap max output tokens and max context</strong> as hard rails to bound the tail.<br><br><strong>The changes that trade off, gated on evals:</strong><br>• <strong>Model routing</strong> — classify difficulty, send the easy majority to a small model. Usually the largest structural saving, and quality-neutral if the router is good. Measure per-route quality, not aggregate.<br>• <strong>Rerank and cut k</strong> — fewer, better chunks cost less <em>and</em> often improve quality by avoiding lost-in-the-middle. One of the rare genuine wins on both axes.<br>• <strong>Fine-tune a small model</strong> for a narrow high-volume task — 10–20x cheaper at equal quality, and the strongest long-term lever.<br>• <strong>Skip chain-of-thought</strong> where evals show it does not help.<br><br><strong>The discipline that makes this safe:</strong> every change runs against the eval set before shipping. Cost optimization without a quality gate reliably produces something cheaper and worse.` },

{ q: 'You are hitting provider rate limits at peak. How do you handle it?', tags: [], a: `<strong>Immediate mitigations:</strong> client-side rate limiting so you shape your own traffic rather than being throttled unpredictably; exponential backoff with jitter (without jitter, retries synchronize and you thunder-herd the provider); and a request queue with priority tiers so interactive traffic is not blocked behind batch work.<br><br><strong>Reduce demand:</strong> aggressive caching (peak traffic often has high query overlap), routing easy requests to a smaller model with separate quota, deduplicating identical in-flight requests, and moving latency-tolerant work to a batch API — which typically has separate, much higher limits <em>and</em> costs less.<br><br><strong>Increase supply:</strong> request a quota increase (usually the actual answer, and often granted quickly); distribute across multiple provider accounts or regions, each with its own limit; add a second provider for overflow; and consider provisioned throughput where the provider offers reserved capacity.<br><br><strong>The architectural fix:</strong> a gateway that manages quota centrally, so one team's burst does not consume the organization's limit and starve everyone else. Without central quota management, rate limits become an unpredictable shared-resource problem.<br><br><strong>Degrade gracefully rather than erroring:</strong> queue with an honest wait estimate, or serve a cached/simpler response with a notice. A 429 surfaced to users is the worst outcome.<br><br><strong>Second-order concern:</strong> retries multiply load exactly when you are already constrained. Cap retry attempts, use circuit breakers, and make sure your retry policy cannot turn a brief throttle into a sustained self-inflicted outage.` },

{ q: 'You depend on one LLM provider. How do you switch without downtime?', tags: ['TRADEOFF'], a: `<strong>Prerequisite: abstract model calls behind a common interface</strong> — your own thin layer or a gateway like LiteLLM. Teams calling a provider SDK directly at a hundred call sites cannot switch at all, so this is the work that must happen first.<br><br><strong>Then validate before switching, because this is the part that is genuinely hard:</strong> prompts do not transfer cleanly across providers. Different models have different instruction-following behavior, tool-calling formats, system-prompt handling, and formatting tendencies. Run your full eval set against the target provider and expect to re-tune prompts — budget for that rather than discovering it during a cutover.<br><br><strong>Account for API differences:</strong> tool-calling schemas, structured-output support, streaming formats, token counting (different tokenizers mean different costs and context limits), and safety-filter behavior all differ. The abstraction layer must normalize these, not just swap a base URL.<br><br><strong>Migration sequence:</strong> shadow (run both, compare offline) → canary at low traffic → progressive rollout with automatic rollback on metric regression. Keep the original provider configured throughout.<br><br><strong>The strategic point worth making:</strong> the goal is not just this migration but ongoing <em>optionality</em>. Maintain at least two validated providers continuously, with evals running against both in CI. That turns provider switching from a project into a config change, and it gives you real failover during outages rather than a theoretical path.<br><br><strong>Watch for lock-in beyond the API:</strong> provider-specific fine-tunes, cached prompt state, and assistant/thread APIs are all migration costs that are easy to accumulate unintentionally.` },

{ q: 'Your system handles 100 req/s but crashes at 5000. How do you scale?', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `<strong>Find the actual bottleneck before scaling anything</strong> — at 50x, the constraint is rarely where teams assume. Instrument connection pools, GPU utilization, vector database latency, memory, and downstream provider limits, then load-test incrementally to find where the curve breaks.<br><br><strong>The usual culprits in order:</strong><br>• <em>Provider rate limits</em> — for API-based systems this is almost always it. Fix by quota increase, multiple accounts/regions, multi-provider distribution, and client-side shaping.<br>• <em>Connection and thread pool exhaustion</em> — a classic: synchronous blocking calls to a multi-second LLM API exhaust the pool at modest concurrency. Async I/O throughout is often the single change that unlocks an order of magnitude.<br>• <em>Unbounded queues</em> — the system does not crash from load, it crashes from memory exhaustion holding queued requests. Add admission control.<br>• <em>Vector database</em> — search latency degrading under concurrency; add replicas.<br>• <em>GPU capacity</em> for self-hosted, where KV cache memory caps concurrency.<br><br><strong>Then scale structurally:</strong> stateless application servers behind a load balancer (state in external stores), horizontal scaling with autoscaling on queue depth, read replicas for vector search, and caching to cut load at the source.<br><br><strong>Design for graceful degradation at the ceiling:</strong> admission control with honest 429s, priority queues, and load shedding of non-critical features. A system that serves 3000 req/s cleanly and rejects the rest is far better than one that accepts 5000 and collapses.<br><br><strong>For self-hosted, remember GPU cold starts are minutes</strong> — pre-scale on forecast, not reactively.` },

{ q: 'A traffic spike brings down your AI system. How do you handle peak traffic?', tags: [], a: `<strong>Admission control is the single most important mechanism.</strong> Most "the spike took us down" incidents are actually unbounded queueing: the system accepts everything, queues grow, memory exhausts or every request times out, and you fail 100% instead of serving 60%. Cap the queue and reject cleanly above it — partial service beats total collapse.<br><br><strong>Add prioritization:</strong> interactive traffic before batch, paying customers before free tier, and critical features before nice-to-haves. Under load, the question is not whether to degrade but <em>what</em> to degrade — decide that in advance, not during the incident.<br><br><strong>Shed load deliberately:</strong> disable expensive optional features (reranking, reflection, multi-hop retrieval), route everything to a smaller model, serve cached responses more aggressively with a looser threshold, and reduce max output length. Feature flags make these one-click during an incident rather than a deploy under pressure.<br><br><strong>Absorb where you can:</strong> queue latency-tolerant work for later processing rather than rejecting it, and use caching — spikes usually have high query overlap, so cache hit rates rise exactly when you need them to.<br><br><strong>Capacity planning is the prevention:</strong> know your peak-to-average ratio and decide explicitly whether to provision for peak (expensive) or degrade (cheaper, requires the mechanisms above). For self-hosted, pre-scale on forecast — GPU cold starts are minutes, so reactive autoscaling arrives after the spike.<br><br><strong>Then test it:</strong> load-test to failure regularly so you know your actual ceiling and can verify that degradation behaves as designed rather than discovering it in production.` },

{ q: 'A provider outage took down your entire system. How do you eliminate single points of failure?', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `<strong>Enumerate the SPOFs honestly</strong> — for most AI systems they are: one model provider, one region, one vector database, and one GPU pool. Then decide which are worth eliminating, since redundancy has real cost.<br><br><strong>Provider redundancy is the distinctive one</strong> and the answer to this specific failure. Abstract model calls behind a common interface, maintain <em>validated</em> prompts for at least two providers, and run your eval set against both in CI. The critical part is validation: an untested fallback silently ships bad output during exactly the incident where you cannot investigate. A failover path you have never exercised is not redundancy.<br><br><strong>Circuit breakers, not just retries.</strong> During an outage, aggressive retries consume your latency budget and make recovery slower. Trip after a failure threshold, route to the fallback immediately, and probe periodically.<br><br><strong>Then design degradation, because full redundancy is not always affordable:</strong> a self-hosted small model as a last-resort tier, cached responses, or retrieval results without generation — which is genuinely useful and has a completely independent failure domain from any LLM provider.<br><br><strong>Beyond the model:</strong> replicate the vector database, spread across regions, and keep conversation state in a replicated store so no single node loss ends a session.<br><br><strong>The organizational fix:</strong> run game days with provider failure injected. Every team believes they have a fallback; most discover during the first real outage that the prompt formatting differs, the tool-calling schema is incompatible, or the API key was never provisioned.` },

{ q: 'Your multi-LLM pipeline fails when one model in the chain breaks. How do you handle orchestration failure?', tags: [], a: `<strong>The core problem is that reliability multiplies.</strong> Five steps at 95% each is ~77% end-to-end. State that arithmetic first, because it reframes the fix: you either shorten the chain or add per-step resilience — you cannot prompt your way out of compounding failure.<br><br><strong>Per-step resilience:</strong> each step gets its own retry policy, timeout, and fallback (a smaller model, a cached result, or a deterministic default). Validate each step's output against a contract before passing it on, so a malformed intermediate is caught where it happened rather than corrupting everything downstream.<br><br><strong>Checkpoint state between steps.</strong> A failure at step 4 should resume from step 3, not restart the pipeline — which requires durable state and idempotent steps with idempotency keys, so a retry after a crash does not duplicate a side effect.<br><br><strong>Design partial success paths.</strong> Ask per step: is this required or enhancing? If the enrichment step fails, can you return the core result without it? Many pipelines fail entirely on an optional step because nobody classified which steps were load-bearing.<br><br><strong>Circuit breakers per step</strong> so a persistently failing component fails fast rather than adding its timeout to every request.<br><br><strong>The structural question worth raising:</strong> can the chain be shorter? Each step is a reliability tax, a latency addition, and a cost. Teams often decompose into six steps for elegance when three would do — and the reliability math makes that expensive.<br><br><strong>Observability requirement:</strong> a single trace spanning all steps, so "the pipeline failed" resolves to a specific step immediately.` },

{ q: 'Your AI pipeline has zero visibility into which step is failing. How do you add observability?', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `<strong>Start with distributed tracing:</strong> one trace per request, with a span per pipeline step — query rewriting, retrieval, reranking, prompt assembly, model call, tool calls, guardrails, output parsing. Each span carries latency, tokens, cost, and status. This alone converts "the pipeline failed" into "step 4 timed out."<br><br><strong>Capture the semantic content, not just timings.</strong> This is what distinguishes LLM observability from conventional APM:<br>• The <strong>fully rendered prompt</strong>, not the template — most "the model ignored my instruction" bugs are actually rendering bugs (empty variable, truncated context, malformed chat template), and they are undebuggable without the literal string sent.<br>• Retrieved chunk ids with scores, so you can distinguish a retrieval failure from a generation failure — the single most useful diagnostic split in RAG.<br>• Tool call arguments and results.<br>• Model name, version, and all sampling parameters.<br>• The prompt/config version id.<br><br><strong>Use OpenTelemetry GenAI conventions</strong> or a purpose-built platform (Langfuse, LangSmith, Phoenix, Braintrust) that adds trace visualization, dataset curation from traces, and eval integration.<br><br><strong>Then close the loop, which is the actual payoff:</strong> any production failure becomes an eval case in one click, and you can replay it against a modified prompt. That loop is the development process for LLM systems, and it is impossible without traces.<br><br><strong>Design in redaction and retention from the start</strong> — traces contain user data, and PII sitting in a trace store is a common, avoidable compliance failure.` },

{ q: 'You quantized your LLM and accuracy dropped. How do you minimize quantization loss?', tags: ['TRADEOFF'], a: `<strong>Quantify the loss on the right benchmark first.</strong> Perplexity is a poor proxy — it barely moves while task performance can drop noticeably, especially on reasoning, math, and long-context tasks. Measure on your actual task eval, and check whether degradation concentrates in a specific category rather than being uniform.<br><br><strong>Then work through the levers:</strong><br>• <strong>Use a better method.</strong> Naive round-to-nearest is much worse than calibrated approaches. <em>AWQ</em> protects salient weight channels identified from activation magnitudes; <em>GPTQ</em> minimizes layer output error using second-order information. Switching from RTN to AWQ or GPTQ often recovers most of the loss for free.<br>• <strong>Improve the calibration set.</strong> GPTQ/AWQ calibrate on sample data — if that data does not resemble your workload, quantization optimizes for the wrong distribution. Use in-domain calibration samples.<br>• <strong>Raise precision selectively.</strong> Keep sensitive layers in higher precision — outlier-heavy layers, the embedding and output layers, and often the first and last blocks. Mixed-precision recovers most quality at a fraction of the memory cost of full precision.<br>• <strong>Try fp8</strong> on H100+ hardware — near-lossless and hardware-native, often the right answer when available.<br>• <strong>Step up a bit width.</strong> 4-bit → 8-bit is essentially lossless; the memory saving is smaller but the quality question disappears.<br>• <strong>QAT</strong> if you can afford a training run — best quality, highest cost.<br><br><strong>The honest framing:</strong> larger models tolerate quantization better than small ones. If you are quantizing a 3B model to 4-bit and quality matters, the tradeoff may simply not be available — use a larger model at 8-bit instead.` },

{ q: 'One failing component takes down your platform. How do you design graceful degradation?', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `<strong>Classify every component as essential or enhancing.</strong> That classification <em>is</em> the design — a system where everything is load-bearing cannot degrade, only fail. Most teams have never done this explicitly, which is why one optional component takes everything down.<br><br><strong>Then build the degradation ladder per feature.</strong> For a RAG assistant: full generation with retrieved context → smaller model → cached response → <em>retrieval results without generation</em> → keyword search → static help content. The retrieval-only step is the underrated one: raw relevant passages are perhaps 70% as useful as a synthesized answer, require no model at all, and have a completely independent failure domain.<br><br><strong>Bulkheads prevent spread:</strong> isolate features into separate resource pools, connection pools, and quotas so a misbehaving agent workload cannot exhaust the shared thread pool and starve the chat path. Without bulkheads, one slow dependency saturates shared resources and everything fails together — that is the actual mechanism behind most "one component took down the platform" incidents.<br><br><strong>Circuit breakers</strong> so a failing dependency fails fast rather than adding its full timeout to every request. Timeouts everywhere, sized to your latency budget.<br><br><strong>Be explicit with users.</strong> A silently degraded response that is noticeably worse destroys trust; a clear "AI assistant is temporarily limited — here are the most relevant documents" is well received.<br><br><strong>Then test it.</strong> Run with each dependency blocked in staging, and inject failures in production via canary. Degradation paths that have never been exercised reliably do not work when needed — that is the whole lesson of this failure.` },
]};
